# Task #374: VOID published log-retention policy enforcement.
#
# Pinned by the "What we log" section on /why and Part V.C of
# /threat-model: the published ceiling is "≤5 days, EFF DNT-compatible".
# This file is the load-bearing config that turns the published claim
# into a fact on disk.
#
# Install on a self-host box as /etc/logrotate.d/void (or include from
# your container's logrotate configuration). The matched glob covers
# both the API server's pino output (if you redirect it to a file) and
# any reverse-proxy access logs that VOID's nginx example writes to
# /var/log/void/. Operators using journald should set
# `MaxRetentionSec=5day` in journald.conf instead — same retention
# ceiling, different file. README-selfhost.md §4f names the choice.
#
# `daily` + `rotate 4` gives a worst-case retention of 5 days (the
# live log plus 4 daily rotations). `maxage 5` is the belt-and-braces
# bound for boxes whose logs roll over by size before time and so might
# otherwise keep a 5+ day old size-rotated file forever.
#
# `compress` reduces residue size but does NOT change retention. `dateext`
# makes the rotated filenames self-describing for audit. `missingok`
# means the file silently disappearing (during cutover, etc.) is not
# an alert. `notifempty` skips rotation of empty files.

/var/log/void/*.log {
    daily
    rotate 4
    maxage 5
    missingok
    notifempty
    compress
    delaycompress
    dateext
    copytruncate
    su root root
}
